## Project Configuration

### Technology Stack

- **Next.js**: Next.js 16 with App Router
- **React**: React 19 with automatic JSX runtime
- **State Management**: Zustand for global client state; server state via Server Components / Route Handlers (TanStack Query is opt-in, not in the default `package.json`)
- **TypeScript**: Strict mode with `@/*` path aliases
- **Routing**: Next.js App Router (file-based)
- **Styling**: Tailwind CSS v4 + shadcn-style UI primitives
- **Testing**: Vitest + Testing Library

## Project Structure

### Folders and Files

- **App Router** in `app/`:
    - `layout.tsx` - Root layout
    - `page.tsx` - Pages (file-based routing)
    - `providers.tsx` - Client providers (analytics init on idle; i18n lives in `NextIntlClientProvider` in `[locale]/layout.tsx`)
    - `loading.tsx` - Loading UI
    - `error.tsx` - Error UI
    - `not-found.tsx` - 404 page
- **Shared** in `shared/`:
    - `ui/` - Shadcn UI primitives (button, input, card, etc.)
    - `lib/` - Utilities and configs (`env`, `logger`, `cspHeader`, `rateLimit`, `utils`, `utils-store`, etc.; i18n config lives in root `i18n/`)
    - `types/` - Shared TypeScript types
    - `constants/` - Shared constants
- **Entities** in `entities/` (business entities, each in its own folder with store/model) — **not in the baseline template**; the directory is created on first domain extraction
- **Features** in `features/` (isolated features with ui/model/api segments) — baseline ships `features/example-form`
- **Hooks** in `hooks/` (organized by domain) — **no `hooks/` directory exists in the baseline**; create it (or keep slice-local hooks under `features/*` / `entities/*`) when the first hook lands

## SEO Configuration

- **Metadata API** - Export `metadata` object in pages/layouts
- **Dynamic Metadata** - Use `generateMetadata` function
- **Sitemap** - `app/sitemap.ts` for automatic sitemap generation
- **Robots.txt** - `app/robots.ts` for robots.txt configuration
- **Open Graph** - Configured in metadata
- **Structured Data** - Add via `metadata.other['application/ld+json']`

### Example

```typescript
// app/page.tsx
import type { Metadata } from 'next';

export const metadata: Metadata = {
    title: 'Home',
    description: 'Welcome',
    openGraph: {
        title: 'Home',
        description: 'Welcome'
    }
};
```

## UI Quality — avoid the "AI aesthetic"

AI-generated UI has recognizable tells. For any UI work reject the generic default in favour of this template's design tokens (`@theme inline` + `:root` / `.dark` HSL vars in `app/globals.css`). This is a public, SEO-facing template — "AI slop" output is a tell and ships into every fork.

- **Palette** — brand is **blue** (`--primary` = `hsl(221.2 83.2% 53.3%)`) on slate neutrals (`--secondary` / `--muted` / `--accent`), `--destructive` red. Use semantic classes (`bg-primary`, `text-foreground`, `bg-muted`, `border-border`, `ring-ring`) — never raw `purple-*` / `indigo-*` or arbitrary hex.
- **Gradients** — none in `@theme`; keep surfaces flat. `tw-animate-css` is the only sanctioned animation layer — no hand-rolled gradient washes or glows.
- **Radius** — tokens derive from `--radius: 0.5rem` (`--radius-sm` / `-md` / `-lg`). Vary radius by hierarchy; don't slap `rounded-2xl` / `rounded-full` on every card and button.
- **Layout** — content-first and priority-driven (this is an SEO surface — the H1 and copy carry ranking weight), not a decorative hero followed by a uniform grid of identical cards.
- **Copy** — realistic strings from `messages/<locale>.json` (next-intl), never lorem-ipsum; real length surfaces wrapping / overflow / truncation during dev.
- **Padding & shadows** — no shadow tokens exist in `@theme`; lean on `shadow-*` sparingly (or `border-border` separation). Use the spacing scale, not blanket oversized padding.
- **Dark mode** — every surface must read in both themes; verify against `.dark` tokens, don't tune light only.
- **Baseline APIs only** — stick to Baseline "Widely available" (`:has()`, container queries, `<dialog>`, `scroll-snap`, view-transitions); don't reach for "Newly available" (e.g. `scheduler.yield()`) without a fallback.

## Accessibility

- **Use semantic HTML tags**
- **Add ARIA attributes** where needed (e.g., `role="status"`, `aria-live="polite"` for loading states)
- **Use `role="alert"` and `aria-live="assertive"`** for critical messages (errors)
- **Ensure keyboard navigation**
- **Use shadcn/ui components** which include accessibility features
- **Don't overuse ARIA** - prefer semantic HTML first

## Internationalization (i18n) — next-intl SSR

- **next-intl App Router SSR** with a `[locale]` route segment; translations render on the server (no client-side JSON fetch, **no `public/locales/*`**).
- **Single source of truth**: `messages/<locale>.json` (flat namespace tree: `common`, `errors`, `home`, `meta`, …).
- **Config** lives under `i18n/` at repo root: `routing.ts` (`defineRouting({ locales: ['en'], defaultLocale: 'en' })`), `request.ts` (`getRequestConfig` messages loader), `navigation.ts` (typed `Link` / `redirect` / hooks), `request-locale.ts` (`requireLocale` narrowing).
- **Locale set is `['en']`** by default — expand only when the caller asks.
- **Type safety**: `global.d.ts` augments next-intl's `AppConfig`; `t('key')` calls are compile-time checked.

### Structure

```
i18n/
  routing.ts        # defineRouting({ locales, defaultLocale })
  request.ts        # getRequestConfig (messages loader)
  navigation.ts     # createNavigation (typed Link / redirect / hooks)
messages/
  en.json           # single source of truth (common, errors, home, meta, …)
```

### Usage in Components

```typescript
// Server Component
import { getTranslations } from 'next-intl/server';

const t = await getTranslations('home');
return <h1>{t('title')}</h1>;
```

```typescript
// Client Component
'use client';

import { useTranslations } from 'next-intl';

const t = useTranslations('home');
return <h1>{t('title')}</h1>;
```

### Adding a New Language

1. Add the code to `locales` in `i18n/routing.ts`
2. Create `messages/<code>.json` (copy `en.json`, translate values)
3. Middleware and `alternates.languages` pick up the new locale automatically
